Confirmed; this is an error in the proof that matters, and the repair is correct and sufficient. Checked by the review (PARI/GP, helper agent, then re-derived by the reviewer):
- ψ₃(ω, t) = −(t² − (6ω² − 4)t − (3ω − 4)), with t₁t₂ = 4 − 3ω and (t₁ − 1)(t₂ − 1) = 15 + 3ω units at 2;
- the resultant of f_t is 256t⁴(t − 1)⁴ = Δ_t², so the potentially-good support has diameter |Δ_t|_v^{1/6};
- the repaired η_v = |2|_v min{…} is ≤ that diameter in the potentially good range and harmless in the bad ranges of Proposition 3.5;
- the cost is log 2 in (7.5).
Two points of the original reason were corrected: supersingularity is not why the 3-torsion lands in the bad disk (prime-to-2 torsion reduces injectively under any good model), and the counterexample alone produces a term divided by |F|. The general argument now in the reason shows why the loss is not O(1/|F|). The same text is in the published version (p.990).
